Every major aviation and nuclear safety regulatory body mandates minimum rest periods because sleep deprivation is treated as a known, quantifiable performance risk.
The performance degradation from sleep loss is dose-dependent and linear — every hour of sleep debt costs measurable points on cognitive tests.
Caffeine mitigates sleepiness but does not restore cognitive performance to rested baseline — you feel more alert but still make more errors.
Adolescents who start school later (delayed 60 minutes) show measurable improvements in test scores, attendance, and mental health outcomes — a natural experiment across hundreds of school districts.
Sleep deprivation disproportionately impairs executive function and emotional regulation, two capacities central to learning, work performance, and healthy relationships.
